---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      What basal metabolic rate measures is the energy your body spends staying alive while
      completely at rest: circulation, breathing, holding temperature, and repairing tissue.
      This basal metabolic rate calculator estimates it with the Mifflin St Jeor equation
      (usually written Mifflin-St Jeor) from your sex, age, height, and weight, then shows what each
      activity multiplier adds on top. The key thing to know is that BMR
      is not what you should eat. It is the resting floor, and total daily energy expenditure, the
      number that answers how much to eat, is BMR multiplied by an activity factor between 1.2 and
      1.9. Finding the resting figure an app used before it applied its own multiplier is a good use
      of this metabolism calculator, which is also called a
      resting metabolic rate calculator.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The BMR Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How BMR Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Even lying still in a dark room, your body is expensive to run. The brain, liver, heart, and
      kidneys account for most of it despite weighing very little, which is why resting energy
      expenditure scales with overall body size rather than with how much you exercise. For most
      adults, resting metabolic rate is 60 to 70 percent of the whole day's burn. That proportion is
      the honest reason "eat less, move more" is mostly a conversation about the smaller slice: your
      activity is real, but it sits on top of a much larger fixed cost you cannot negotiate with.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mifflin-st-jeor">
    <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor Equation <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The formula is 10 times weight in kilograms, plus 6.25 times height in centimetres, minus 5
      times age in years, then plus 5 for men or minus 161 for women. Weight is the dominant term,
      height comes second, and age subtracts slowly over a lifetime. The sex term is a flat offset of
      166 calories, which is why two people of identical size get different figures. Published in
      1990, the Mifflin-St Jeor equation replaced the 1919 Harris-Benedict equation because
      Harris-Benedict tends to overestimate resting energy for modern body compositions by around 5
      percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is how the equation is built, and it explains what a normal BMR looks like at a
      given size and age: there is no single normal figure, because the dominant term is your body
      mass. A 30 year old man of 175 cm and 70 kg lands near 1,650 calories a day; a woman of the
      same age at 165 cm and 60 kg lands near 1,320. A figure that looks low next to someone else's
      usually means you are smaller or older, not that your metabolism is broken.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bmr-vs-tdee">
    <h2>BMR Versus TDEE <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The two get confused constantly, and the confusion is expensive. BMR is rest; total daily
      energy expenditure is rest plus everything else, including the energy spent digesting food.
      A sedentary day multiplies BMR by 1.2, a moderately active one by 1.55, and hard daily training
      by 1.9. Eating at your BMR while living a moderately active life is a deficit of several hundred
      calories a day, which is a far more aggressive cut than most people mean to make. The
      calculator shows all five multipliers at once so the size of that choice is visible before you
      commit to one.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="BMR is a floor, not a target">
      <p>Set your eating target against maintenance, and treat basal metabolic rate as the line you do not cut below without medical supervision. A slow metabolism is rarely the reason weight will not move; a misjudged activity multiplier usually is.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>Man, 30, 70 kg, 175 cm.</strong> For example, BMR is about 1,649 calories a day, roughly 69 calories every hour spent doing nothing at all.</p>
    <p><strong>Woman, 30, 60 kg, 165 cm.</strong> For example, BMR is about 1,320 calories. The gap to the case above is mostly size, with 166 calories of it coming from the sex term.</p>
    <p><strong>Same man, moderately active.</strong> For example, multiplying 1,649 by 1.55 gives roughly 2,556 calories, so activity is adding over 900 calories to the resting figure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Eating at BMR instead of maintenance">
      <p>
        This is the single most common misreading. Eating your BMR means eating nothing for the
        activity you actually did, which produces a much steeper deficit than intended, poor training
        quality, and the rebound that follows. Use the maintenance figure as the reference point.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting the formula to see your muscle">
      <p>
        Muscle burns roughly 13 calories per kilogram a day at rest against about 4.5 for fat, so
        composition does matter. The equation works only from height, weight, age, and sex, so a
        lean, muscular person will be underestimated and someone carrying more fat slightly
        overestimated.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="An estimate, not a measurement">
      <p>Measuring resting metabolic rate properly needs indirect calorimetry in a lab. Two people the same weight get different BMR figures in real life for reasons a formula cannot see. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
